  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was Father's Day\u2014a structurally depressed Monday that typically runs 15% below baseline\u2014and yet Port Washington delivered $3,357, up 39% versus the holiday-adjusted comparable. This is a genuine operational win, not calendar noise. You're running hot: five groomers at $478/groomer-day (just 4.6% below your trailing-30-day $500 baseline despite the holiday headwind), add-on attach improved to 58% from 50%, and retail climbed 29%. Month-to-date Port is at $63,727 against a DOW-adjusted expectation of $63,564\u2014you're +0.3% and outpacing last May. Hicksville, however, is a different story. One active groomer yesterday (Julia B) produced only $335, 15.6% below the $397 trailing-30-day baseline, and today you've got just two groomers scheduled with zero bathers across the next three days. Hicksville's visit-cadence drift is concerning: the 12+-week gap bucket grew from 8.4% to 10.2%, a leading indicator customers are stretching out their appointments. This isn't a Monday anomaly\u2014it's a capacity and retention problem that needs immediate attention.\n\nThe bright spot: Port's year-to-date is up 51% in revenue and 52% in appointments versus last year. That's the benchmark you want.\n\nThe watch: Hicksville needs groomer hours and a reactivation plan for that 58-person lapse pool worth $40K annually.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Port has 6 groomer-days scheduled today and 6 tomorrow with 7 bather hours; Hicksville drops to 2 today and 1 tomorrow with zero bathers; next 7 days Port runs 29 groomer-days and Hicksville runs only 10, widening the capacity gap."
